PG.notes("l216", "<p class=\"summary\">Conceptual intro to version control with Git: commits act as save points of a project that you can compare against or restore. Previews the module: local Git on the command line, then cloning, forking, pull requests and merging.</p>\n<h3>Version control as save points</h3>\n<ul><li>Putting a file under version control with Git lets you record snapshots of it over time. Each snapshot is a version (in Git terms a commit), for example version 1 after the first few lines and version 2 after more work.</li><li>If later changes wreck the code, you can diff the broken working copy against any earlier snapshot to see what changed, or roll back to a saved version.</li><li>You can return to any recorded version, not only the most recent one, as long as you know which one you want.</li><li>Rollback matters most when code is interdependent (many files or classes relying on each other), where a bad change can be hard to untangle by hand.</li></ul>\n<h3>Module roadmap</h3>\n<ul><li>Git is used from the command line: local version control first (next lesson).</li><li>Later topics: GitHub remotes, cloning repositories, branching and merging, forking and pull requests.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Git only keeps versions you explicitly commit; unsaved edits between commits cannot be recovered from Git.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l217", "<p class=\"summary\">A terminal demo of local Git: create a folder with text files, git init, stage with git add, save with git commit -m, inspect with git status and git log, compare with git diff, and discard bad edits with git checkout. Introduces the vocabulary of working directory, staging area and local repository.</p>\n<h3>Setup used in the demo</h3>\n<ul><li>Uses command-line basics from the earlier terminal module: cd to move into a folder, mkdir to create one, ls to list contents, touch to create an empty file.</li><li>Demo project: a folder called Story on the desktop with chapter1.txt, chapter2.txt and chapter3.txt, each edited in a text editor. The files are only stand-ins for source code.</li><li>ls -a also lists hidden entries, which is how the .git folder becomes visible (Finder hides it).</li></ul>\n<h3>Three places a change can live</h3>\n<ul><li>Working directory: the folder where git init was run (here Story). Git compares what is in this folder with what has been recorded.</li><li>Staging area: a holding zone where you select which changes go into the next save point. Files can sit in the working directory without being staged.</li><li>Local repository: the history of commits stored in the hidden .git folder on your machine.</li><li>Flow: edit files in the working directory, git add to stage, git commit to record in the repository.</li><li>Why staging exists: it lets you commit only some of your files or edits, leaving others out of the record.</li></ul>\n<h3>Commands in order</h3>\n<ul><li>git init: turns the current directory into a repository by creating the hidden .git folder. Prints a message that an empty repository was initialised.</li><li>git status: shows the state of files. Untracked files (in the working directory, not yet staged) show in red; staged new files show in green under changes to be committed. Modified tracked files appear as modified.</li><li>git add &lt;file&gt;: stage one file, e.g. git add chapter1.txt.</li><li>git add . : stage everything in the current directory in one go; the dot means 'here'. Saves typing one name at a time.</li><li>git commit -m &quot;message&quot;: record the staged changes as a save point; -m supplies the message inline. Without -m Git opens an editor for the message.</li><li>Commit messages should say what changed since the previous save point. Convention is the present tense, as in 'Complete chapter 1' or 'Initial commit' for the first one, as if you were submitting the change now.</li><li>git log: lists commits newest first, each with a unique hash identifying it, author, date and the message. The label HEAD marks the commit you are currently on.</li><li>git diff &lt;file&gt;: compares the file in the working directory with the last committed version. Removed lines are shown in red and added lines in green, so you can inspect or copy useful parts before deciding.</li><li>git checkout &lt;file&gt;: discards uncommitted changes to that file and restores it to its state in the last commit. In the demo, a chapter overwritten with gibberish is restored instantly.</li></ul>\n<h3>Demo walk-through</h3>\n<ul><li>Commit 1: chapter1.txt is staged and committed with the message 'Complete chapter 1'.</li><li>Commit 2: chapter2.txt and chapter3.txt are created and filled, git status shows them untracked, git add . stages both, and git commit -m 'Complete chapter 2 and 3' makes the second save point. git log now shows two commits with different hashes.</li><li>Rollback demo: chapter3.txt is deliberately ruined and saved; git status reports it as modified but unstaged; git diff chapter3.txt shows the damage; git checkout chapter3.txt brings back the committed text.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In current Git versions git restore &lt;file&gt; is the dedicated command for discarding working-tree changes, and git checkout &lt;file&gt; still works for it. git checkout -- &lt;file&gt; is a safer spelling if a branch has the same name as the file.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> git checkout only restores changes that were never committed; once changes are committed you move between versions using their hashes (for example git checkout &lt;hash&gt; or git revert).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Full local workflow from the demo</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd ~/Desktop\nmkdir Story\ncd Story\ntouch chapter1.txt     # then edit it in an editor\ngit init                # creates the hidden .git folder\nls -a                   # shows .git\ngit status              # chapter1.txt is untracked (red)\ngit add chapter1.txt    # stage it\ngit status              # now a new file to be committed (green)\ngit commit -m &quot;Complete chapter 1&quot;\n\ntouch chapter2.txt chapter3.txt   # then edit both\ngit add .\ngit commit -m &quot;Complete chapter 2 and 3&quot;\ngit log                 # two commits, HEAD on the latest</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Inspecting and discarding changes</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">git status                # shows chapter3.txt modified, not staged\ngit diff chapter3.txt     # lines removed vs added since last commit\ngit checkout chapter3.txt # restore last committed version\n# modern equivalent:\ngit restore chapter3.txt</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running git init in the wrong folder (for instance the home directory), making everything inside it part of the repository.</li><li>Forgetting git add before git commit; unstaged changes are not included in the commit.</li><li>Past-tense or vague commit messages such as 'stuff', which make the log useless.</li><li>git checkout &lt;file&gt; permanently discards uncommitted edits, so run git diff first if anything in the file might be worth keeping.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Git does not track empty folders and ignores nothing by default; add a .gitignore for files you do not want recorded.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l218", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to publish a local Git repository to GitHub: create an empty repository in the browser, link it with git remote add origin, upload the history with git push -u origin main, and view commits on GitHub. Recaps the local pipeline (working directory, staging, local repo) and where the remote fits.</p>\n<h3>Remote repositories and GitHub</h3>\n<ul><li>A remote repository is a copy of the project hosted on another machine; GitHub is the hosting service used in the course. It is free; create an account at github.com and confirm the email address.</li><li>Create a repo in the browser: use the + menu at the top right, choose New repository, give it a name (the demo reuses the local folder name, Story) and an optional description.</li><li>Visibility: Public means anyone can see every committed file; Private restricts access. Public repos are useful for learning from others' code and for contributing to open-source projects.</li><li>Leave 'initialize with a README' unticked when pushing an existing local repo, so the remote starts empty and there are no conflicting commits.</li><li>After creation, GitHub shows setup instructions. The command-line route (push an existing repository) is the one used in the lesson; the GitHub Desktop client is an alternative the instructor does not use.</li></ul>\n<h3>Connecting local to remote</h3>\n<ul><li>Run commands from inside the local project folder (the working directory). git log first confirms which commits exist, two in the demo.</li><li>git remote add origin &lt;URL&gt;: registers a remote called origin pointing at the GitHub repository URL copied from the page. The name is arbitrary, but origin is the standard convention and recommended so others recognise it.</li><li>git push -u origin main: uploads the commits of the local main branch to the remote named origin. The -u flag (upstream) links the local branch to the remote one so later you can simply run git push or git pull.</li><li>main is the default branch: the linear chain of save points. Branches are covered in a later lesson.</li><li>Success is signalled by the prompt returning and a message that the local main now tracks the remote main from origin. Upload time depends on repository size.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Older Git setups may create the first branch as master; git branch -M main renames the current branch so it matches GitHub's default. GitHub's setup page lists this command too.</li></ul>\n<h3>Viewing the result on GitHub</h3>\n<ul><li>Refreshing the repo page shows all the files with their latest commit messages.</li><li>The Commits list shows each save point and its time; opening one shows exactly which lines changed in that commit.</li><li>Insights then Network (the graph view) draws the main branch with one node per commit; hovering shows each message.</li><li>Hosting history on GitHub also helps for non-code projects, such as writing, since any earlier version can be seen or restored.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mental model</h3>\n<ul><li>Local flow: working directory, git add into the staging area, git commit into the local repository, building a main branch timeline.</li><li>Remote: a parallel repository on GitHub. Local and remote can differ; git push sends local commits to the remote, which is what syncs them.</li><li>In the demo the local repo is the .git folder in Story and the remote is the GitHub project that stores all the commits.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Push an existing local repo to a new empty GitHub repo</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd ~/Desktop/Story\ngit log                                            # confirm the local commits\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/&lt;user&gt;/Story.git\ngit branch -M main                                 # only if your branch is not already called main\ngit push -u origin main                            # upload commits and link the branches</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Useful follow-up commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">git remote -v      # list configured remotes and their URLs\ngit push           # later pushes need no arguments after -u\ngit pull           # fetch and merge new commits from origin</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Ticking 'add a README' on GitHub before pushing an existing project, which leads to a rejected push because the histories differ.</li><li>Pasting the wrong URL or running the commands outside the project folder.</li><li>Anything committed to a public repository is public, including secrets; the next lesson (.gitignore) addresses this.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Authentication: pushing over HTTPS requires a personal access token or a credential helper rather than the account password.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l219", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why and how to keep files out of Git using a .gitignore file: secrets, OS and editor settings, and generated folders such as node_modules. Demonstrates ignoring individual files and patterns, undoing an accidental git add with git rm --cached, and starting a Node project from GitHub's pre-made .gitignore template.</p>\n<h3>What should not be committed</h3>\n<ul><li>Secrets: passwords, API keys, cloud credentials. Once pushed to a public remote they can be harvested almost immediately, with real financial damage possible.</li><li>Personal or machine-specific files: editor settings, OS metadata such as macOS .DS_Store (a hidden file storing things like icon arrangement in a folder). They are useless to collaborators and clutter the repository.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Generated or downloadable content such as node_modules, build output and logs; these can be recreated with npm install or a build.</li></ul>\n<h3>Creating and writing .gitignore</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a plain file named exactly .gitignore (leading dot, lowercase) in the project root, e.g. with touch .gitignore. It is hidden, so use ls -a to see it. Git looks for this exact name.</li><li>One pattern per line. To ignore a single file, write its name exactly, matching case: .DS_Store, secrets.txt.</li><li>A line starting with # is a comment.</li><li>Wildcards: *.txt ignores every file ending in .txt anywhere under the repository. Use with care, since it will exclude files you actually want.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A trailing slash such as logs/ matches directories; a leading ! re-includes a path that an earlier rule excluded.</li><li>The .gitignore file itself is normally committed so that collaborators share the same rules.</li></ul>\n<h3>Demo sequence</h3>\n<ul><li>Project folder with file1.txt, file2.txt, file3.txt and a secrets file, plus a .DS_Store, and an empty .gitignore.</li><li>git init, then git add . stages everything; git status shows the unwanted secrets and .DS_Store would be committed.</li><li>To undo that staging: git rm --cached -r . removes everything from the staging area (index) while leaving the files on disk; -r makes it recursive. git status then shows nothing staged.</li><li>Add .DS_Store and secrets.txt to .gitignore, run git add . again and check git status: only .gitignore and the three file1-3 files are staged; the ignored ones no longer appear.</li><li>Committing and pushing now includes only those four files.</li></ul>\n<h3>Templates for Node projects</h3>\n<ul><li>GitHub maintains a public repository named gitignore (under the github organisation) with ready-made templates per language and framework. The Node template already covers node_modules, logs, environment files and similar.</li><li>Copy the template contents into your .gitignore and add your own extras, for instance a '# Project-wide' comment followed by .DS_Store.</li><li>Flow shown: new Node project, npm install, create .gitignore with the template, then git init, git add ., git status, git commit -m 'Initial commit'.</li><li>Use .gitignore for API keys and other sensitive data before the first commit; list the files holding them (for instance .env).</li></ul>\n<h3>Important limitation</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> .gitignore only affects untracked files. A file that was already committed stays tracked and in history after you add it to .gitignore; remove it from tracking with git rm --cached &lt;file&gt;. A secret that was ever pushed should be treated as leaked and rotated, because it still exists in history.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Ignoring files in a fresh project</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd ~/Desktop/Project\ntouch .gitignore\nls -a                  # .gitignore and .DS_Store visible\ngit init\ngit add .              # oops: secrets.txt and .DS_Store got staged\ngit status\ngit rm --cached -r .   # unstage everything (files stay on disk)\n# edit .gitignore, then:\ngit add .\ngit status             # secrets and .DS_Store no longer listed\ngit commit -m &quot;Initial commit&quot;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">.gitignore syntax</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\"># Project-wide\n.DS_Store\nsecrets.txt\n\n# Logs\n*.log\n\n# Node\nnode_modules/\n.env</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Wrong name or case (gitignore, .Gitignore); Git silently ignores it.</li><li>Adding a file to .gitignore after it was committed or staged; it keeps being tracked until removed with git rm --cached.</li><li>Overly broad wildcards like *.txt that exclude files you meant to keep.</li><li>Pushing secrets to a public repository before setting up .gitignore.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> git rm --cached -r . without a path argument unstages everything; it does not delete files from disk.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l220", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains git clone as the way to copy a remote GitHub repository, with its full commit history, into a local working directory. Demonstrates cloning and running two open-source JavaScript projects (a browser Quake port and a Wordle clone) and points to beginner-friendly projects for first open-source contributions.</p>\n<h3>What git clone does</h3>\n<ul><li>git clone &lt;url&gt; downloads a remote repository: every file, every commit and the whole version history, into a new folder on your machine.</li><li>The result is a complete local repository. You normally clone once, then keep working on your own branches and pull or push afterwards.</li><li>It is the reverse of the previous lesson's push: push sends local commits to a remote, clone brings a remote down for the first time.</li><li>The URL is the repository's GitHub address (https form ending in .git, copied from the green Code button).</li></ul>\n<h3>Why clone someone else's project</h3>\n<ul><li>Customise an open-source program to your own needs.</li><li>Extend it with features it lacks.</li><li>Fix a bug you found.</li><li>Self-host free alternatives to paid tools (project management, newsletter, time tracking); curated lists of such self-hosted apps exist on GitHub.</li><li>Reading and modifying other people's code is one of the fastest ways to improve as a developer.</li></ul>\n<h3>Workflow: clone, install, run</h3>\n<ul><li>Clone the repo in a terminal, then cd into the new folder it created.</li><li>Read the repo's README for run instructions; JavaScript projects usually need npm install to fetch dependencies listed in package.json.</li><li>Some projects need extra setup commands (the Quake demo needed a content-server setting copied from its README before starting).</li><li>Start the app as the README says: node &lt;file&gt; or npm start. The terminal prints the local address (e.g. port 8080 for Quake) to open in the browser.</li><li>Because the code is now on your disk you can rename things, change colours, or alter game rules and re-run.</li></ul>\n<h3>Examples used</h3>\n<ul><li>QuakeJS: the classic Quake game compiled to run in a browser, served by a small Node server on port 8080. Use the exact URL the README specifies.</li><li>Word Mastermind: a Wordle clone in plain JavaScript, HTML and CSS. After npm install and npm start it opens on a local address. The dict folder holds the word lists (including other languages) and the src folder holds the game logic.</li><li>The motivation for the clone was to play more than one word per day, which the official game forbids.</li></ul>\n<h3>Next step: contributing to open source</h3>\n<ul><li>The awesome-for-beginners repository lists projects that welcome first-time contributors.</li><li>Contributing requires branches, merging and pull requests, covered in the following lessons.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Clone, install and run a Node-based project</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">git clone https://github.com/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;.git\ncd &lt;repo&gt;\nnpm install\nnpm start</pre>\n<h2>The task: Clone and run an open-source repo</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A terminal with git, Node and npm installed.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Clone a public JavaScript project from GitHub and run it locally, then make a small change.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Pick a project such as the Wordle clone or QuakeJS and copy its clone URL from GitHub.</li><li>Run git clone with that URL, then cd into the folder.</li><li>Follow the README: npm install, then any extra commands, then npm start or node.</li><li>Open the printed local address in the browser.</li><li>Browse the source and change something small (title, colours, word list).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The game runs in the browser from your local copy and your small edit shows up.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to cd into the cloned folder before npm install.</li><li>Skipping README setup steps, so the app starts but fails to work.</li><li>Opening a different URL from the one the README specifies when the app is tied to it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l221", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces branches as parallel lines of commits that let you experiment without touching the main project, and merging as the way to bring a finished experiment back. Walks through a full local example with a story repo: create a branch, commit on it, switch back, commit on main, merge, push, and view the result in GitHub's network graph.</p>\n<h3>Why branches exist</h3>\n<ul><li>A branch is a separate line of commits that starts from some existing commit. Main keeps receiving stable updates while an experimental branch receives work on a new feature or idea.</li><li>Both lines can advance at the same time without affecting each other.</li><li>On large projects several branches are normally active at once (features, bug fixes), because unfinished work can break the project and should not land on main until it works.</li><li>When the experiment succeeds, merge it into main. Git checks for conflicts, and if there are any they are resolved by editing before the merge completes. Hosted services expose this as a merge/pull request.</li></ul>\n<h3>Branch commands</h3>\n<ul><li>git branch &lt;name&gt; creates a new branch at the current commit. It does not switch to it.</li><li>git branch with no argument lists local branches; the asterisk marks the branch you are on. Use it whenever you are unsure where you are.</li><li>git checkout &lt;name&gt; switches branches. The working-directory files change to match that branch, so edits made on one branch are absent on the other.</li><li>git add . then git commit -m &quot;message&quot; record work on whichever branch is checked out. Keeping add and commit as separate steps is recommended while learning, although they can be combined.</li><li>git log shows the history reachable from the current branch, so the log differs per branch.</li></ul>\n<h3>Merging</h3>\n<ul><li>To merge branch X into main: first git checkout main, then git merge X. The merge always goes into the branch you are currently on.</li><li>If the branches have diverged, git creates a merge commit and opens the default text editor (Vim) for the commit message. Leaving the message as is and typing :q! quits without changes and finishes the merge with the default message (the lecture's flow). Typing :wq is the usual save-and-quit alternative.</li><li>After merging, the feature branch still exists; merging does not delete it.</li><li>git log on main afterwards shows the earlier commits plus the merge commit.</li><li>Publishing the result: git push -u origin main. The -u flag sets the upstream so later pushes and pulls need no arguments.</li></ul>\n<h3>Seeing it on GitHub</h3>\n<ul><li>The repo's commit count rises to include the branch commit, the new-file commit and the merge commit.</li><li>Insights then Network shows a graph of the branch splitting off main and rejoining at the merge.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Full branch and merge sequence from the lecture</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">git log                       # see current history\ngit branch alien-plot         # create branch\ngit branch                    # list branches, * marks current\ngit checkout alien-plot       # switch to it\n# edit chapter1.txt and chapter2.txt\ngit add .\ngit commit -m &quot;modify chapter 1 and 2 to have alien theme&quot;\ngit checkout main             # back to main: files revert\n# create chapter4.txt\ngit add .\ngit commit -m &quot;add chapter 4&quot;\ngit merge alien-plot          # Vim opens for merge message\n# type :q! to leave the editor\ngit push origin main -u</pre>\n<h2>The task: Practise branching with your own repo</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A local git repository with a few text files (the lecture uses a story with chapter files), optionally linked to a GitHub remote.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a feature branch, commit on it, commit separately on main, merge, and push.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create a repo (local and on GitHub) holding a story or poem split into files.</li><li>Create a branch with git branch and switch with git checkout.</li><li>Edit files and commit on the branch.</li><li>Switch back to main and confirm the edits are not there.</li><li>Add a new file on main and commit it.</li><li>Merge the branch into main and push with -u.</li><li>Open Insights then Network on GitHub to view the graph.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Main contains both the branch's edits and the new file, the log shows a merge commit, and GitHub's network graph shows the split and rejoin.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Merging from the wrong branch: run the merge while on the branch that should receive the changes.</li><li>Getting stuck in Vim after git merge; exit with :q! (or :wq to save).</li><li>Assuming the remote already has local commits; only pushed commits appear on GitHub.</li><li>Editing files while on the wrong branch because you did not check git branch first.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer git also offers git switch &lt;name&gt; and git switch -c &lt;name&gt; as clearer alternatives to checkout.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l222", "<p class=\"summary\">Short optional pointer to an interactive Git tutorial for going deeper into topics not covered in the course, such as cherry-picking and rebasing.</p>\n<h3>Optional extra practice</h3>\n<ul><li>The course points to the free browser-based Learn Git Branching site (learngitbranching.js.org) for visual, level-by-level Git challenges.</li><li>It covers advanced topics beyond the course, including cherry-pick and rebase.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Cherry-pick copies a single commit onto the current branch; rebase replays a branch's commits on top of another base to produce a linear history.</li><li>Entirely optional; not required for later sections.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Learn Git Branching challenges (optional)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A web browser; no install needed.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Work through the interactive levels to practise branching, merging, cherry-pick and rebase.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open learngitbranching.js.org.</li><li>Complete the introductory levels, then the advanced ones on rebase and cherry-pick.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> More confidence manipulating commit history.</p>\n</div>");
PG.notes("l223", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains how strangers collaborate on a repository they cannot write to: fork it into their own account, change their copy, then open a pull request asking the owner to merge. Demonstrated end to end with two GitHub accounts, then illustrated with a large open-source library's pull request history.</p>\n<h3>The four areas and the collaboration problem</h3>\n<ul><li>Recap of the four areas: working directory, staging area, local repository, remote repository.</li><li>The owner of a remote repo has write access. Giving write access to everybody online would invite unwanted or incompatible changes, so write access is reserved for trusted people, such as members of the same team.</li><li>Teammates with read and write rights can clone, commit, push and resolve conflicts directly. Everyone else needs another route: fork plus pull request.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fork versus clone</h3>\n<ul><li>git clone copies a repository from GitHub to your local machine.</li><li>A fork copies a repository from one GitHub account to another, entirely on GitHub. The fork includes all files and all earlier commits.</li><li>You own the fork and have full permissions on it. You can then clone your fork locally, change it, commit and push to it.</li><li>Forking is done with the Fork button on the original repo's page; the copy appears under your username.</li><li>Commits made on the fork do not show on the original repository. In the original's Insights then Network graph, the fork appears as a separate branch, and the Members/Forks view lists who forked it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pull requests</h3>\n<ul><li>A pull request (PR) is a proposal asking the original owner to take your changes. It is called a pull request because the owner, who has write rights, pulls the changes in, rather than the contributor pushing to a repo they cannot write to.</li><li>Create one from your fork (Contribute / Pull request, or the prompt GitHub shows). GitHub reports whether the branches can merge automatically and shows a diff of the changed lines.</li><li>Fill in a title and description explaining what you changed and why, then press Create pull request.</li><li>Your proposed changes will not appear in the original repo until the owner merges them.</li><li>The owner sees a counter on the Pull requests tab, can read the description, inspect the commits and file differences, leave review comments and approve.</li><li>Pressing Merge pull request (optionally with a message) and confirming adds the changes as a new merge commit on the main repo; the network graph then shows the fork's branch merging back.</li></ul>\n<h3>Contributing in the real world</h3>\n<ul><li>Popular projects have thousands of forks and hundreds of closed PRs. Contributions range from tiny (README updates) to code additions and fixes.</li><li>Good PRs document the goal, implementation details and tests performed. Maintainers often request tweaks before approving.</li><li>A good first contribution: take a library you already use, fix a bug or add a feature, and offer it back via a fork and PR.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Typical contributor flow (commands run locally after forking on GitHub)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">git clone https://github.com/&lt;you&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;.git\ncd &lt;repo&gt;\ngit checkout -b my-change\n# edit files\ngit add .\ngit commit -m &quot;Describe the change&quot;\ngit push -u origin my-change\n# then open a pull request on GitHub</pre>\n<h2>The task: Fork a repo and open a pull request</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A public GitHub repository you do not own (the lecture used a second account to fork the story repo).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Fork the repo, change a file in your copy, and open a pull request to the original.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Sign in with the contributing account and find the target repository.</li><li>Click Fork so the copy appears under your account.</li><li>Edit a file on your fork (for example a chapter) and commit with a clear message.</li><li>Check the Network graph to see your commit as a separate branch.</li><li>Open a pull request with a title and explanation, and create it.</li><li>As the owner, review the PR under Pull requests, comment, then Merge pull request and confirm.</li><li>Check the owner's Network graph to see the merge.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The PR appears in the owner's list, and after merging the original repo contains the contributor's commit and a merge commit.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing fork with clone.</li><li>Expecting to push straight to someone else's repository; without write access only a PR is possible.</li><li>Expecting commits on the fork to show in the original before the owner merges.</li><li>Sending PRs without a description of what and why.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The lecture commits to master; many repos now use main as default branch, and contributors usually work on a separate branch of their fork.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l224", "<p class=\"summary\">Study tip on spaced repetition: knowledge sticks when reviewed at growing intervals, and cramming fades fast. Recommends revisiting concepts regularly and practising by building small things yourself.</p>\n<h3>Spaced repetition for programming</h3>\n<ul><li>Learning a concept once and never using it again leads to forgetting; revisit it after increasing gaps (for example a day, a week, a month, then longer).</li><li>The course repeats concepts on purpose; if it feels repetitive, raise the playback speed to review faster.</li><li>Keep a personal list of learned concepts and schedule reviews of each.</li><li>Cramming gives short-lived memory; spaced review turns knowledge into a lasting skill.</li><li>Practise by writing small programs or quizzes, or rebuilding a simple course project from scratch alone. Hands-on work matters more than watching lectures.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Cramming everything once and expecting it to last.</li><li>Only watching videos without writing code.</li></ul>");
